# Python 3 support in noweb: the fib example

fib.nw is a literate program that demonstrates noweb's Python 3
support.  It defines two root chunks, <<[[fib.py]]>> and
<<[[Makefile]]>>, so the Fibonacci program and its build file are
tangled from the same literate source, and each is woven with its own
syntax highlighting (Python and Make).

It exercises three pieces of noweb:

- autolang: annotates each chunk with its language (noweave
  -autolang), so the autodefs filter below skips non-Python chunks
  and the index stays free of foreign identifiers.
- autodefs.python3: finds Python definitions automatically, for the
  identifier index and cross-references (noweave -autodefs python3).
- tominted: typesets each code chunk with the minted LaTeX package,
  choosing the lexer per chunk (noweave -filter tominted).

## Requirements

- noweb built with the Icon tools (for autodefs.python3)
- Python 3 and Pygments (for tominted/minted)
- LaTeX with the minted package; compile with pdflatex -shell-escape

fib.nw loads minted through the noweb package, with
`\usepackage[minted]{noweb}` in its preamble, so there is no separate
`\usepackage{minted}` line to remember.  When noweb generates the
preamble instead (a weave without `-delay`), pass `noweave -option
minted` to get the same effect.  The `-minted` shorthand bundles that
option with `-filter tominted`, so `noweave ... -index -minted fib.nw`
is enough for a highlighted weave.

## Building

Highlighted PDF (definitions index and chunk cross-references):

```bash
noweave -delay -autolang -autodefs python3 -index -filter tominted \
    fib.nw > fib.tex
pdflatex -shell-escape fib.tex
pdflatex -shell-escape fib.tex
```

Compiling this weave needs the one-time whitelist setup of the next
section, because tominted uses the bundled custom lexer by default;
weave with `-filter 'tominted -nolexer'` to skip it.

Classic noweb rendering, with identifier *uses* hyperlinked inside
code (minted owns the code body in the highlighted version, so use
links inside code exist only in this mode):

```bash
noweave -delay -autolang -autodefs python3 -index fib.nw > fib-xref.tex
pdflatex -shell-escape fib-xref.tex
pdflatex -shell-escape fib-xref.tex
```

The examples' Makefile wires both: `make fib.tex fib-xref.tex`.

## Linked references inside docstrings

In the highlighted PDF, chunk references stay hyperlinked even
*inside* a Python string (like the docstring chunk in fib.nw).
Pygments' escape mechanism refuses to work inside string tokens,
so tominted renders references through a bundled custom lexer by
default: it locates the installed noweb_lexer.py next to its own
script and writes that absolute path into the woven document, so
no copy of the lexer file is needed next to the LaTeX sources.

This needs one-time setup, because minted treats loading custom
lexer files as arbitrary code execution. Whitelist the lexer by
hash in `~/.config/latexminted/.latexminted_config` (or
`~/.latexminted_config`):

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/latexminted
printf '{"custom_lexers": {"noweb_lexer.py": "%s"}}\n' \
    "$(sha256sum ../src/lib/noweb_lexer.py | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
    > ~/.config/latexminted/.latexminted_config
```

The key is the file's name and hash, not its path, so one entry
covers the file wherever noweb is installed; the hash must be
updated whenever noweb_lexer.py changes. To skip the setup
entirely, weave with `-filter 'tominted -nolexer'`: references
inside strings are then shown as literal `<<...>>` text.

## Tangling

The chunk names include the brackets, and make needs real tabs, so:

```bash
notangle -R'[[fib.py]]' fib.nw > fib.py
notangle -t8 -R'[[Makefile]]' fib.nw > Makefile
```

(`-t8` copies tabs through; by default notangle expands them to
spaces, which breaks make.)  `make fib-run` in this directory tangles
both into the fib/ subdirectory and runs the tangled Makefile's test
target.

## What autodefs.python3 indexes

By default (module-level interface):

- top-level `def` and `async def` functions: `fib`, `main`
- top-level classes
- top-level simple assignments `NAME = value`: `DEFAULT_COUNT`

With `-local` (noweave -filter 'autodefs.python3 -local' -index), it
additionally indexes methods and nested functions.  Function-local
variables, tuple unpacking, attribute assignments and annotated
assignments are never indexed.

Note that the index of fib.pdf also lists `PYTHON` and `COUNT` from
the Makefile chunk: -autodefs applies to every code chunk, and make's
variable assignments match the Python assignment pattern.
